# How That Looks on a Man

You have a face that works in the foyer. It asks about the sermon. It finds the man who sits alone and means the handshake. Then you're home, and the weather in your voice is a different country. Same shirt. Same ring. Two climates.

That's how sin wears on a man. It doesn't always look like a wreck. It looks like a finished surface. It looks like competence. Sin wears different clothes on us than it does on anyone else. If you don't know the shapes, you'll walk past them in the glass every morning and call the man there fine.

## The shapes it takes

Here are the shapes I've seen most often. One or two will be yours.

The first is the locked drawer. Every man is tempted to keep a second set of books: an account she doesn't know, a screen, a friendship that isn't what he says it is, or his real fears, which he's decided nobody gets to see. The drawer starts as a boy's wish for privacy and grows into a man's hidden room. Everything bad that ever happens to a man happens in that room.

The second is the fortress. Ask him how he's doing and he's fine. Ask what he needs and he needs nothing. He's the corner post for everybody and he leans on no one. He calls that strength. It isn't. It's pride in a work shirt. The fortress looks like the second mark, accepting responsibility, and that's what makes it hard to spot. A man who accepts responsibility can still be helped. The fortress can't. It was built to keep out exactly the thing the man needs.

The third is the scoreboard. The quiet ranking of every man in the room by his truck, his job, his wife, his kids, his body, his salary. A man who's always measuring can never be glad for another man's win and can never rest in his own. Saul had this, and it drove him to try to kill the young man who'd just won a battle for him.

The fourth is the podium. Sin dressed as righteousness, and Jesus hated it more than any other kind. Generous when people are watching. Patient in the church lot and a storm in the cab on the way home. Jesus said such men have already been paid in full: all the reward they're ever going to get, from people who don't really know them.

The fifth is the throne. The man who has to win every conversation in his house, by volume or by silence or by sarcasm that leaves marks. He tells himself he's leading. His wife would tell you she's managing him. His kids have learned to read the truck in the driveway. Scripture doesn't soften it.

:::margin
Colossians 3:19 (NIV)
"Husbands, love your wives and do not be harsh with them."
:::

God saw it coming. He put a fence around it in a single sentence.

The sixth is contempt, the throne's quieter brother. The sneer. The eye roll at his wife's story. The disdain for the softer man at church or the son who cries. A man who looks down at people can't look up at God. It's the same neck.

The seventh is the recliner. Sloth dressed as rest. On a man it doesn't look like laziness. It looks like a fellow who's earned his evening, and it costs his house the rafters.

## The man on the roof

There's a man in the Bible who wore about half of those shapes at once, in the span of a single spring. He was the best man of his generation. If it could take David it can take you. It starts with passivity.

:::margin
2 Samuel 11:1 (NIV)
"In the spring, at the time when kings go off to war, David sent Joab out with the king's men and the whole Israelite army. They destroyed the Ammonites and besieged Rabbah. But David remained in Jerusalem."
:::

That's what kings did, and it's what David had always done. That year he sent Joab and stayed home, and nobody would have called it sin. He'd earned a rest. He was a man with nothing in his hands, walking the roof in the evening, and you know what happens on a roof like that.

He sees her. He asks about her. Someone tells him she's married. He sends for her anyway. Every mark inverted in a single afternoon. A real man rejects passivity, accepts responsibility, leads courageously, and expects the greater reward, God's reward. David didn't reject passivity; he'd been drifting since the army left. He didn't accept responsibility; he'd handed the war to Joab and his conscience to his appetite. He didn't lead courageously; he used power, the coward's substitute for courage, and took what he wanted from a woman who couldn't say no to a king. He wasn't living for God's reward. He was living for the next hour.

Then the locked drawer, because she's pregnant. He calls her husband home, gets him drunk, and tries to pass the child off. Uriah won't do it; he's too honorable to sleep in a bed while his brothers are in the field. David sends him back with a sealed order to put him where the fighting is worst and pull back. Uriah dies. David marries the widow. Everything is covered.

:::margin
2 Samuel 11:27 (NIV)
"After the time of mourning was over, David had her brought to his house, and she became his wife and bore him a son. But the thing David had done displeased the LORD."
:::

The kingdom kept running. The wars kept getting won. Nobody knew. David ran Israel for the better part of a year with a murdered man in his drawer and a stolen wife in his house, and he was good at it. Competence is not righteousness. A man can run a kingdom, a church, a household, while he rots. The surfaces stay clean while the man does not.

Psalm 32 is David describing that year from the inside: bones wasting away, a hand heavy on him. That's what a locked drawer does to a man even when the drawer is working.

## What exposed him

It wasn't his conscience. David didn't wake up and decide to come clean. God sent a man.

:::margin
2 Samuel 12:7 (NIV)
"Then Nathan said to David, 'You are the man! This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: I anointed you king over Israel, and I delivered you from the hand of Saul.'"
:::

You are the man. Four words. Nathan told him a story about a rich man who stole a poor man's only lamb. David got furious. Nathan said: that's you. A brother walked into the throne room and named it to his face. That's what it took, and it's almost always what it takes. Sin on a man is rarely exposed by the man. It's exposed by a wife who finally says it, or a brother who won't take fine, or a statement in the mail, or the hand of God getting so heavy the man can't stand.

Which is why you need a Nathan, and why you need to be one. Brothers around a table exist so somebody can say four words in your throne room.

Look at the sentence that mattered.

:::margin
2 Samuel 12:13 (NIV)
"Then David said to Nathan, 'I have sinned against the LORD.' Nathan replied, 'The LORD has taken away your sin. You are not going to die.'"
:::

I have sinned against the LORD. Five words, and no but. No she came out on the roof. No Uriah should have gone home. No I'm the king and I've carried a lot. The sentence stops at the period, which is what a man's confession does, and the forgiveness comes in the same breath. There were consequences, terrible ones. His standing was restored the moment the drawer was opened.

## Checking the glass

You're not going to see these shapes by looking harder. Sin is deceitful. You'll need tests.

The drawer test asks what you wouldn't want your brother to see. Phone, history, accounts, calendar, the friendship. If there's a drawer, there's a problem, whatever's in it. The drawer itself is the problem. A man with nothing hidden has nothing to defend, and a man with nothing to defend is free.

The no test asks when you last let somebody tell you no and accepted it. If you can't remember, you're on a throne.

The wife test is the bravest one. If you're married, ask her, in a quiet moment, where she sees you hiding, and where you're harsh. Then don't argue. Listen, and thank her. If you're single, ask your closest brother or your mother the same thing. Somebody has been watching.

The podium test asks what you do only when someone's watching. Where's the gap between the lot and the cab, between Sunday morning and an unwatched night? Whatever's in that gap is what you're performing.

The throne test asks who in your house is afraid of you. Not who respects you. Who is afraid. If the answer isn't no one, get off the throne and onto your knees in front of the people who've been reading your weather.

## The shapes in different houses

If you're single, the drawer and the podium are your largest risks. Unwatched hours. A strong incentive to look good at church. Get a brother with the passwords and a habit of asking. Kill the drawer before it holds anything worth hiding.

If there's a baby in your house, watch the throne. Exhaustion makes a man harsh, and harshness gets rationalized fast when you've barely slept. Your wife is more tired than you are. Apologize the same night, every time.

If you sit the divorced seat, the scoreboard is going to call to you. Comparing your house to hers, your new life to her new life, your holiday table to theirs. Every comparison is a small theft of your own peace. Put the scoreboard down. You've got a Father who sees in secret, and He isn't keeping that score.

If you're older, look hard at the fortress. Men farther on have usually become very good at needing nothing, and it has cost them. The hardest question at a table of brothers is what do you need from us. Some of those men have never been asked. When they find an answer, the table changes.

## The Man with no drawer

Jesus had no drawer. Nothing He hid from the Father, nothing He managed, nothing He performed. When they came to arrest Him, He said He had spoken openly to the world. When they searched for a charge, they had to invent one. He was fully known, all the way down, and unashamed.

That's what He's making you into. Not a man with a cleaner surface. A man with no locked drawer, whose inside and outside match, who can be seen by his wife and his brothers and his God without flinching, because the thing that used to need hiding has been confessed and forgiven.

Open the drawer. Let a Nathan in. Say the five words.

## Before you turn

You've probably found your shape, and it isn't comfortable. David found his only when a man said four words, and the five words he said back restored his standing in a single breath. The Man you're following had no drawer. He isn't asking for a cleaner foyer face. He's asking you to let a Nathan in. May the Father give you a brother who will say you are the man, and the honesty to say I have sinned without a but. A man with nothing to defend is free.

